    {
      "id": 17592186050845,
      "mathAcademyId": 1424,
      "title": "The Zero Product Rule for Solving Quadratic Equations",
      "uuid": "6b2f9405-d642-57d9-b6da-adccbbb39242"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186069945,
      "id": "44b39091-c3ce-5da7-935a-b6a8e7f88bf6",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Suppose we have a quadratic equation like\n$x^2-25 = 0$.\nWe can solve this equation using the **difference of squares** formula, shown below:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}a}^2 - {\\color{blue}b}^2 = ({\\color{red}a}+{\\color{blue}b})({\\color{red}a}-{\\color{blue}b})\n$$\n\nUsing the difference of squares formula, we can factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 25 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 5)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nx+5=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-5=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-5 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-5,5$. We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm 5$.\n\n**Note:** We could have solved the equation $x^2-25=0$ using the square root method. However, the factoring method introduced in this lesson will become very useful later when we move on to more complicated quadratic equations. Make sure that you learn it!",
      "mathAcademyId": "1623",
      "stepId": "17592186069840",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068484,
      "id": "387068d5-e460-551c-9a0f-ef1602e7f7f9",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068877,
        "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations"
      },
      "markdown": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $a^2 - 100 = 0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1413",
      "stepId": "17592186069841",
      "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations",
      "workedSolution": "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} - 100 &= 0 \\\\\n(a)^{2} - (10)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(a - 10)(a + 10) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\na-10=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a+10=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\na=10 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a=-10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\na = -10, 10\n$$\n\nWe can write this even more concisely as $a=\\pm 10$."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068485,
      "id": "7e0d8a37-ec5c-5b5c-a24c-b05472a5add4",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068878,
        "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $4z^2 - 25 = 0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1414",
      "stepId": "17592186069842",
      "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients",
      "workedSolution": "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4z^{2} - 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(2z)^{2} - (5)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(2z - 5)(2z + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2z-5=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2z+5=0\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 2z+5 & =0 \\\\ 2z & =5 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 2z & =-5 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & z & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nz = -\\dfrac 5 2, \\dfrac 5 2\n$$\n\nWe can write this even more concisely as\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\dfrac 5 2\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068488,
      "id": "393a0e1c-9694-5613-967a-4569b3f7a14f",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068879,
        "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations by Reorganizing and Factoring"
      },
      "markdown": "For which values of $t$ is $-2t^2 -16 = -5 -3t^2$?",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1416",
      "stepId": "17592186069843",
      "title": "Solving Perfect Square Quadratic Equations by Reorganizing and Factoring",
      "workedSolution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2t^{2} - 16 &= -5 - 3t^{2} \\\\\n(-2t^{2} + 3t^{2}) + (-16 + 5) &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - 11 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - 11 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - (\\sqrt{11})^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(t - \\sqrt{11})(t + \\sqrt{11}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nt-\\sqrt{11}=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad t+\\sqrt{11}=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{11} \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad t=-\\sqrt{11}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nt = -\\sqrt{11}, \\sqrt{11}\n$$\n\nWe can write this even more concisely as\n\n$$\nt=\\pm \\sqrt{11}\n$$"
    }
  ],
